1
00:03:23,571 --> 00:03:29,571
If you think your family's different, wait 'til
you meet the family of the future.

2
00:06:47,142 --> 00:06:53,142
Meet the Robinsons is a animation, comedy, family
film directed by Stephen J. Anderson.

3
00:10:10,714 --> 00:10:16,714
Lewis, a brilliant young inventor, is keen on creating a time machine
to find his mother, who abandoned him in an orphanage.

4
00:13:34,285 --> 00:13:40,285
Things take a turn when he meets Wilbur Robinson and his family.

5
00:16:57,857 --> 00:17:03,857
Lewis (voice), played by Daniel Hansen.

6
00:20:21,428 --> 00:20:27,428
Lewis (voice), played by Jordan Fry.

7
00:23:45,000 --> 00:23:51,000
Wilbur (voice), played by Wesley Singerman.

8
00:27:08,571 --> 00:27:14,571
Michael 'Goob' Yagoobian (voice), played by Matthew Josten.

9
00:30:32,142 --> 00:30:38,142
Bowler Hat Guy / Grandpa Bud / Tallulah (voice), played by Stephen J. Anderson.

10
00:33:55,714 --> 00:34:01,714
Cornelius Robinson (voice), played by Tom Selleck.

11
00:37:19,285 --> 00:37:25,285
Meet the Robinsons is a 2007 American animated science fiction comedy film produced by Walt Disney
Animation Studios, based on the 1990 children's book A Day with Wilbur Robinson by William Joyce.

12
00:40:42,857 --> 00:40:48,857
The film was directed by Stephen Anderson, and written by Anderson, Don Hall, Nathan Greno, Joe Mateo, Jon Bernstein, Michelle Spitz, and Aurian Redson, and stars the voices of Daniel
Hansen, Jordan Fry, Wesley Singerman, Angela Bassett, Tom Selleck, Harland Williams, Laurie Metcalf, Nicole Sullivan, Adam West, Ethan Sandler, Tom Kenny, and Anderson.

13
00:44:06,428 --> 00:44:12,428
It follows an orphaned 12-year-old inventor,
Lewis, who is desperate to be adopted.

14
00:47:30,000 --> 00:47:36,000
He meets Wilbur Robinson, a young time-traveler who takes him
to the year 2037 to visit his eccentric family.

15
00:50:53,571 --> 00:50:59,571
They must prevent a mysterious bowler-hatted man from
changing Lewis's fate, and, by proxy, the future.

16
00:54:17,142 --> 00:54:23,142
Development began in 2002, with production starting in 2004 under the working title A
Day with Wilbur Robinson, with a release initially slated for 2006.

17
00:57:40,714 --> 00:57:46,714
Anderson developed a personal connection to the main character, Lewis,
as he himself grew up an orphan and was later adopted.

18
01:01:04,285 --> 01:01:10,285
Disney originally planned to adopt Joyce's style, but it was decided to slightly rework
the style due to his involvement stylistically in Blue Sky Studios' Robots.

19
01:04:27,857 --> 01:04:33,857
The animation crew had the challenge to animate CG humans,
being inspired by Pixar's The Incredibles.

20
01:07:51,428 --> 01:07:57,428
Inspiration for the film came from Disney animated classics, such as Alice in
Wonderland, Cinderella, and Peter Pan, as well as Warner Bros.

21
01:11:15,000 --> 01:11:21,000
Cartoons, to capture a 1950s aesthetic.

22
01:14:38,571 --> 01:14:44,571
Disney's acquisition of Pixar in early 2006 led to nearly 60% of the film,
including the villain and the ending, being scrapped and reworked.

23
01:18:02,142 --> 01:18:08,142
Danny Elfman composed the film's score, while artists such as Rufus Wainwright, Rob Thomas, The
All-American Rejects, and They Might Be Giants contributed to its soundtrack.

24
01:21:25,714 --> 01:21:31,714
Meet the Robinsons premiered at the El Capitan Theatre in Los Angeles on March
25, 2007, and was released in the United States on March 30, 2007.

25
01:24:49,285 --> 01:24:55,285
The film received generally positive reviews from critics but was a box office
disappointment, grossing $170.5 million against a budget of $150 million.

26
01:28:12,857 --> 01:28:18,857
The film explores themes of future, friendship, flying car,
saving the world, based on novel or book.

27
01:31:36,428 --> 01:31:42,428
The tone is lighthearted and thought_provoking.
